# $NetBSD$
#
# Make the module reachable from /usr/lib.  libc dlopen()s name service
# modules by bare soname, and the run-time linker restricts a set-user-ID
# program to the trusted directories, so a module living only under
# ${PKG_PREFIX} would never load for su(1), login(1) and friends.
#
# This is a template fragment, not a script of its own: pkgsrc concatenates it
# into +INSTALL after mk/pkginstall/header, which has already taken the stage
# out of the arguments and left it in ${STAGE}.  Testing $2 here would compare
# against nothing at all.

STNS_MODULE="nss_stns.so.@NSS_VERSION@"

case ${STAGE} in
POST-INSTALL)
	${LN} -sf "${PKG_PREFIX}/lib/${STNS_MODULE}" "/usr/lib/${STNS_MODULE}"

	${CAT} <<STNS_MESSAGE

To start resolving accounts through STNS:

1. Write @PKG_SYSCONFDIR@/stns.conf.  An example is installed as
   ${PKG_PREFIX}/share/examples/nss_stns/stns.conf.

2. Add stns to /etc/nsswitch.conf, after files so that local accounts
   always win and the machine stays usable if the API is unreachable:

	passwd: files stns
	group:  files stns

   The default shipped by NetBSD is "compat"; either replace it as above,
   or leave it and add stns to passwd_compat and group_compat.

A symbolic link to the module has been created in /usr/lib, without which
su(1) and login(1) would silently fail to resolve STNS accounts.

STNS_MESSAGE
	;;
esac
